## Why This Is Asked

Assess ability to ground a feature in a measurable business case, translating product intuition into concrete cost and risk items for a mobile offline component.

## Key Concepts

- Offline-first architecture
- Inverted index vs suffix structures
- Delta sync and storage budgets
- Privacy/compliance and consent
- Rollout strategy and go/no-go criteria

## Code Example

```javascript
// Minimal indexing sketch (pseudo)
function indexDocs(docs) {
  const idx = {};
  for (const d of docs) {
    for (const t of tokenize(d.text)) {
      if (!idx[t]) idx[t] = [];
      idx[t].push(d.id);
    }
  }
  return idx;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you measure energy consumption during indexing and constrain it?
- How would you design delta-sync conflicts and eventual consistency across devices?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests the ability to design and quantify edge ML systems under privacy and connectivity constraints, balancing energy, latency, and regulatory risk. It probes real-world trade-offs of federated learning with differential privacy in production environments.

## Key Concepts

- **Federated learning with local updates**: Devices train on local data and share model updates instead of raw data.
- **Secure aggregation and differential privacy**: Cryptographic protocols and noise injection protect individual contributions.
- **Energy and memory budgeting**: Quantifies the compute cost of on-device training.
- **Drift detection and validation**: Monitors model quality under privacy-preserving constraints.
- **Rollout strategy and offline fallback**: Ensures reliability when connectivity is intermittent.

## Code Example

```javascript
// Simple local update sketch for FL
function localUpdate(weights, localData, learningRate) {
  const gradient = computeGradient(weights, localData);
  return weights.map(w => w - learningRate * gradient);
}

// Secure aggregation (conceptual)
async function aggregateUpdates(updates, privacyBudget) {
  const clipped = updates.map(u => clipGradient(u, MAX_NORM));
  const noised = addNoise(clipped, privacyBudget);
  return average(noised);
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to build a holistic CBA for an in-car edge AI feature, balancing real-time compute, OTA updates, data privacy, and regulatory compliance, while accounting for drift, validation, and fault tolerance.

## Key Concepts

- Fleet-level TCO across devices and software layers
- Edge compute energy and thermal constraints
- OTA bandwidth, update cadence, rollback strategies
- Privacy, data minimization, regulatory logs
- Drift monitoring, validation pipelines, go/no-go criteria

## Code Example

```javascript
// Simple illustrative cost model for 18-month CBA
function tco18m({fleetSize, perVehicleStateGB, energyW, otaMinorMB, otaMajorMB, driftCost, validationCost, rollbackCost, upliftPerMonth}) {
  const hardwareCost = fleetSize * perVehicleStateGB; // placeholder units
  const monthlyEnergyCost = fleetSize * energyW * 24 * 30; // approximate energy cost in Wh per month
  const otaCost = fleetSize * (otaMinorMB * 0.001 + otaMajorMB * 0.01); // hypothetical cost units
  const drift = fleetSize * driftCost;
  const validation = fleetSize * validationCost;
  const rollback = fleetSize * rollbackCost;
  const total = hardwareCost + monthlyEnergyCost + otaCost + drift + validation + rollback;
  const breakEvenMonths = total / upliftPerMonth;
  return { total, breakEvenMonths };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you validate the drift model and set driftCost thresholds? 
- What metrics tie CBA to safety/regulatory requirements and how would you incorporate them? 
- How would you handle market/regulatory changes affecting privacy costs?

## Why This Is Asked

Evaluates ability to design a cost-aware, privacy-preserving XAI feature with on-device constraints and compliance.

## Key Concepts

- On-device explainability budgets, latency targets, and data locality
- Telemetry storage limits, retention, and privacy controls
- Cost trade-offs between local inference vs cloud-audit

## Code Example

```javascript
function capLogs(logs, maxBytes) {
  // Simplified capping by character length
  let acc = '';
  for (const s of logs) {
    if ((acc + s).length <= maxBytes) acc += s;
    else break;
  }
  return acc;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you handle model drift and auditability across OS updates?
- What metrics would you track for go/no-go decisions and how would you set thresholds?

